cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  XRSpinoPelvic1K: amodal vertebral endplate landmarks on lateral
  radiographs, learned from 3-D CT
message: If you use this software or dataset, please cite it.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Schwing
    given-names: Gregory
    affiliation: Wayne State University
    orcid: https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000
repository-code: https://github.com/Gregory-Schwing-MD-PhD/XRSpinoPelvic1K
license: Apache-2.0
version: 1.0.0
keywords:
  - spine
  - radiograph
  - digitally reconstructed radiograph
  - spinopelvic parameters
  - pelvic incidence
  - amodal segmentation
  - landmark localization
abstract: >-
  On a lateral spinal radiograph the sacral ala superimposes on the S1 vertebral
  body, so the S1 endplate cannot be recovered from what is visible: fitting it
  from the projected silhouette is 26-32 degrees in error at every slab fraction
  tested, because the medial-lateral coordinate that separates ala from body does
  not survive projection. XRSpinoPelvic1K renders digitally reconstructed
  radiographs from segmented CT at randomised oblique angles and projects the
  ground truth through the identical geometry, yielding amodal vertebral corner
  landmarks -- the true extent of each endplate, including the part occluded on a
  real film. Endplates fitted in 3-D and projected reproduce sacral slope and
  lumbar lordosis to under one degree. Published manual inter-rater reliability
  for the sacral endplate is as low as ICC 0.41, attributed to the same overlap,
  making human agreement rather than a segmentation benchmark the meaningful
  comparison.
